{
 "summary": "`compactnessCounterexample_bigO` proves the existence of a finite nonempty family F of connected, bipartite, non-acyclic graphs with two properties: a uniform c>0 with ex(n,F) ≥ c·n^{4/3} eventually for every F∈F, and ex(n,F) = O(n^{4/3−1/48}). It also derives that F is not compact and that the corrected Erdős–Simonovits compactness conjecture fails. `not_erdos_180` states the disproof of the conjecture directly. The most important caveat is `quantitativeCompactnessCounterexample`: it is quantified ∀ over all families and all c, C rather than ∃, so it asserts every family and every pair of reals satisfies the whole conjunction. That is literally false (for example family = ∅ or c = 0), so it is not a faithful rendering of Theorem 1.1. A kernel-checked proof of it would indicate a problem.",
 "glossary": [
  {"decl": "FiniteGraph", "line": 10, "plain": "A finite simple graph on vertex set Fin order.", "standard_concept": "finite simple graph", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": ""},
  {"decl": "FamilyFree", "line": 14, "plain": "The host on n vertices contains no member of the family as a (not necessarily induced) subgraph.", "standard_concept": "F-free", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": "Uses Mathlib SimpleGraph.Free, i.e. not IsContained (injective homomorphism)."},
  {"decl": "familyExtremal", "line": 18, "plain": "ex(n,F): the maximum number of edges of an F-free graph on Fin n, with sup over the empty set = 0.", "standard_concept": "extremal number of a family", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": "The empty graph on n vertices is F-free whenever F has no 0-vertex or edgeless member, so the sup is a true max."},
  {"decl": "IsCyclicFamily", "line": 24, "plain": "Every member of F contains a cycle.", "standard_concept": "family of graphs each containing a cycle", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": ""},
  {"decl": "IsCompactFamily", "line": 27, "plain": "There exist F∈F and C>0 with ex(n,F) ≤ C·ex(n,F) for all sufficiently large n.", "standard_concept": "property (1) of the paper", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": ""},
  {"decl": "CompactnessConjectureStatement", "line": 33, "plain": "Every finite nonempty family all of whose members contain cycles is compact.", "standard_concept": "corrected Erdős–Simonovits compactness conjecture", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": ""},
  {"decl": "extremalScale", "line": 37, "plain": "n^{4/3}", "standard_concept": null, "matches_standard": "yes", "note": ""},
  {"decl": "UniformMemberLower", "line": 40, "plain": "For every F∈F, eventually c·n^{4/3} ≤ ex(n,F).", "standard_concept": "ex(n,F)=Ω(n^{4/3}) for each F∈F", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": "The constant c is uniform over the family; this is equivalent for a finite family (take the minimum)."}
 ],
 "lean_claims": [
  {"theorem": "not_erdos_180", "line": 46, "plain": "There is a finite nonempty family of graphs, each containing a cycle, that is not compact."},
  {"theorem": "quantitativeCompactnessCounterexample", "line": 52, "plain": "For EVERY finset of graphs F and EVERY reals c, C, all of the following hold. F is nonempty. Every member is connected, bipartite and contains a cycle. c>0 and C>0. Each member has ex ≥ c·n^{4/3} eventually. Every F-free host on n vertices has e^16 ≤ C·n^21. ex(n,F)^16 ≤ C·n^21 for all n. 1/48>0 and 21/16 = 4/3 − 1/48. F is not compact. The compactness conjecture fails."},
  {"theorem": "compactnessCounterexample_bigO", "line": 72, "plain": "There exist a finite nonempty family F of connected bipartite graphs each containing a cycle, and c>0, with three properties. Every F∈F has ex(n,F) ≥ c·n^{4/3} eventually. ex(n,F) = O(n^{4/3−1/48}) as n→∞. F is not compact. Hence the compactness conjecture is false."}
 ],
 "paper_claims": [
  {"ref": "Theorem 1.1", "plain": "There exists a finite nonempty family F of connected bipartite graphs, each containing a cycle, with ex(n,F)=O(n^{4/3−1/48}) and ex(n,F)=Ω(n^{4/3}) for each F∈F. In particular no member satisfies (1), disproving the compactness conjecture."},
  {"ref": "Section 1.3 (upper bound)", "plain": "ex(n,F) = O(n^{21/16})."},
  {"ref": "Theorem 1.2", "plain": "There is a connected bipartite 2-degenerate H with ex(n,H) ≥ c·n^{3/2+ε} for all sufficiently large n."}
 ],
 "coverage": [
  {"paper_ref": "Theorem 1.1", "lean_theorem": "compactnessCounterexample_bigO", "relation": "equivalent", "why": "Existential statement with matching hypotheses, the O-bound, the per-member Ω-bound and the non-compactness consequence."},
  {"paper_ref": "Theorem 1.1 (in particular)", "lean_theorem": "not_erdos_180", "relation": "equivalent", "why": "Negation of the corrected compactness conjecture, matching (1)."},
  {"paper_ref": "Theorem 1.1 / Section 1.3 quantitative form", "lean_theorem": "quantitativeCompactnessCounterexample", "relation": "different", "why": "Universally quantified over family, c and C instead of existentially, so the statement is false as written."},
  {"paper_ref": "Theorem 1.2", "lean_theorem": null, "relation": "not_formalized", "why": "Covered elsewhere (sibling file J_TwoDegenerateGraphs)."}
 ],
 "flags": [
  {"id": "F1", "category": "quantifier", "severity": "high", "decl": "quantitativeCompactnessCounterexample", "line": 53, "paper_ref": "Theorem 1.1", "explanation": "The statement begins `∀ (family) (c C : ℝ)` and its body is a pure conjunction, so it claims every finset of graphs is nonempty, every c is positive, and so on. This is refutable: family = ∅ violates `family.Nonempty`, and c = 0 violates `0 < c`. The paper's claim is existential (∃ F, c, C). As written the theorem is false, so a kernel-accepted proof of it would signal an inconsistency or a mis-specified challenge. It does not formalize the paper's quantitative claim.", "polarity": "lean stronger", "probe_idea": "theorem probe : ¬ (∀ (family : Finset FiniteGraph) (c C : ℝ), family.Nonempty ∧ ...) := fun h => by simpa using (h ∅ 1 1).1"},
